course: biology
lesson: 312
title: Whales by kind, the baleen whales: blue, fin, humpback, grey, right and minke
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: The biggest whales have no teeth. Instead, a curtain of baleen plates hangs from the upper jaw, made of keratin, the same stuff as your fingernails.
explain: A baleen whale gulps a huge mouthful of sea, then pushes the water out through the baleen, and the krill and small fish stay inside like pasta in a sieve.
explain: The blue whale may be the biggest animal that ever lived, up to about 30 metres long, with a heart as heavy as two grown-ups. Yet it feeds on animals smaller than your little finger.
explain: Each kind has its own trick: humpbacks blow nets of bubbles, grey whales suck mud from the seabed, fin whales race like greyhounds, and every whale's spout has its own shape.
fact: The blue whale may be the biggest animal that has ever lived, up to about 30 metres long; its heart weighs about 180 kilograms and on a dive can beat as slowly as two beats a minute, a study reported.
fact: Baleen is a curtain of hundreds of plates on each side of the upper jaw, made of keratin like fingernails; it sieves krill and small fish out of the water.
fact: The fin whale is the second biggest animal, about 20 to 25 metres long, and so fast it is called the greyhound of the sea; its lower jaw is white on the right side and dark on the left.
fact: Humpbacks blow a spiral net of bubbles around a shoal of fish and then swim up through the middle with mouths open; often several work together.
fact: Humpbacks have the longest flippers of any whale, about a third of their body length, with a row of bumps on the front edge that helps them turn tightly; engineers have copied the bumps for fan and turbine blades.
fact: Grey whales swim about 16,000 to 20,000 kilometres there and back each year, feeding in the Arctic in summer and having babies in warm lagoons in Mexico in winter.
fact: Grey whales feed by sucking mud from the seabed and sifting out small creatures; in Mexican lagoons some now come up to boats and let people touch them, though whalers once called them devil fish.
fact: Right whales have rough white patches on their heads covered in tiny whale lice; the pattern differs on each whale, so scientists recognise each one, and only about 370 North Atlantic right whales were left, reported.
fact: The minke is the smallest of the big baleen whales, about 7 to 10 metres long, and is the whale most often seen near boats around Britain in summer.
fact: Baleen whales have two blowholes and toothed whales have one; blowholes are nostrils that moved to the top of the head over millions of years.
fact: Each kind's spout has its own shape: a right whale's blow is V-shaped, so whale-watchers can name a whale from its spout.
fact: Whales dive to eat and come up to poo, and their poo, rich in iron, feeds tiny floating plants near the surface, which feed krill; so more whales can mean more krill, not less.
exercise: What is baleen made of? | Keratin, like your fingernails | Bone, just like your teeth and jaw | Shell, like a crab or a lobster | Wood, like the branches of a tree
exercise: How does a baleen whale catch krill? | It gulps water and pushes it out through the baleen, keeping the krill in | It bites each krill with its sharp teeth | It stings the krill with its flippers | It sucks the krill up through its blowhole and swallows the water and krill together
exercise: About how long can a blue whale grow? | About 30 metres | About 3 metres long | About 300 metres | About 10 metres long
exercise: About how heavy is a blue whale's heart? | About 180 kilograms | About 2 kilograms, like a bag of sugar | About 18 kilograms, like a dog | About 18 tonnes, like a lorry
exercise: Which whale is called the greyhound of the sea? | The fin whale, because it is long and fast | The minke, because it barks like a dog | The grey whale, because it is grey | The right whale, because it chases dogs along the shore
exercise: What is odd about a fin whale's lower jaw? | It is white on the right side and dark on the left | It has teeth on one side only | It is shorter than its upper jaw by a whole metre or more | It glows in the dark
exercise: How do humpbacks use bubbles to catch fish? | They blow a ring of bubbles around a shoal and swim up through it | They blow bubbles to hide from killer whales | They trap all the fish inside one single giant bubble and swallow it | They use bubbles to float while asleep
exercise: What do the bumps on a humpback's flipper do? | Help the flipper turn tightly in the water | Hold its babies on its back | Store extra food for the long winter | Make loud songs by rubbing the bumps together
exercise: How long are a humpback's flippers? | About a third of its body length | About as long as your arm | Twice as long as its whole body, tail and all | About 10 centimetres
exercise: Where do grey whales have their babies? | In warm lagoons in Mexico | Under the thick Arctic ice | In the wide rivers of Europe | On sandy beaches out of the water
exercise: How do grey whales feed? | They suck mud from the seabed and sift out small creatures | They hunt seals on the ice | They eat seaweed from rocks | They filter only sunlight through their skin and need no other food at all
exercise: What are the white bumpy patches on a right whale's head? | Rough skin covered in tiny whale lice | Snow that never melts | Barnacles painted on by scientists | Old scars from fighting great white sharks
exercise: How do scientists recognise each right whale? | The pattern of its head patches is different on every whale | Each one wears a numbered tag from birth | They all sing their own name, which the scientists can record | By counting their teeth
exercise: Which is the smallest of the big baleen whales? | The minke | The blue whale | The fin whale | The humpback
exercise: How many blowholes does a baleen whale have? | Two | One | Three | None
exercise: What are blowholes really? | Nostrils that moved to the top of the head | Ears that let sea water in when the whale dives | Mouths for drinking fresh water from the rain | Holes for letting out sound only, for singing
exercise: How does whale poo help the sea? | It feeds tiny floating plants, which feed krill | It cleans sharks' teeth | It makes the water warmer | It sinks and slowly builds new islands on the sea floor
test: Count them: a whale-watching boat sees 3 humpbacks and 2 sperm whales blow at once. Sperm whales have one blowhole. How many blowholes are open? | 8 | 5 | 10 | 6
test: Count them: 4 blue whales swim past. How many flippers and how many back legs do they have together? | 8 flippers and no back legs | 8 flippers and 8 back legs | 4 flippers and 4 back legs | 16 flippers and no back legs
test: In Pinocchio a huge whale swallows a man and his boat whole. What would a real blue whale do? | It could not; it feeds on krill and its throat is far too narrow | It would swallow boats every day | It would chew the boat into pieces with its huge teeth | It would keep the man as a pet inside
test: «whales eat all the krill, so kill whales = more fish for us, simple» What is the truth? | Whale poo feeds the plants that feed krill, so fewer whales can mean less krill | True, whales are the only thing eating krill in the whole ocean | True, krill grow faster with no whales | Whales do not eat krill at all
test: From a boat you see a spout shaped like a V. Which whale is it most likely to be? | A right whale | A minke | A fin whale | A goldfish in a bowl
test: What would happen to a grey whale baby born in the icy Arctic instead of a warm lagoon? | It would struggle more, being thin and losing heat in the cold water | Nothing at all, babies like cold best and grow faster | It would grow baleen faster | It would turn into a polar bear
test: «mum, why's the fin whale's face two colours, is it ill» What is the best answer? | No, its jaw is naturally white on the right and dark on the left | Yes, it has a sunburnt side | It painted itself with mud to scare the boats away | Only old fin whales go two colours
test: A fan company copies bumps from a whale's flipper. Which whale did they copy? | The humpback | The blue whale | The minke whale | The grey whale
test: «ugh whale with nits lol» What is true about right whales? | Tiny whale lice live on its rough head patches, and the patterns help name each whale | Lice only live on sick whales about to die | Whales catch lice from people on boats who lean over the side to look at them | The white patches are paint from ships
test: A child says baleen is just big teeth. What is right? | No, baleen is keratin plates like fingernails, used as a sieve | Yes, they are teeth that fell out | Yes, baleen is bone, like the tusk of an elephant or a walrus | No, baleen is made of shell
test: Count it: a grey whale swims 18,000 kilometres a year for 10 years. How far is that? | 180,000 kilometres | 18,000 kilometres | 1,800 kilometres | 28,000 kilometres
exam: Identify the material of which the baleen plates of a whale are composed. | Keratin, the substance of which human fingernails are composed | Enamel, the substance of which human teeth are composed | Calcium carbonate, the substance of which shells are composed | Cartilage, the substance of which the human ear is composed
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the feeding method of the humpback whale. | Individuals may encircle a shoal with a spiral of bubbles and ascend through its centre | Individuals capture seals upon the sea ice by stealth | Individuals feed exclusively upon seaweed attached to rocks | Individuals pursue prey into rivers far inland
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the annual movements of the grey whale? | It travels some sixteen to twenty thousand kilometres, feeding in the Arctic and calving in Mexican lagoons | It remains throughout the year within a single bay | It travels from the Arctic to the Antarctic and calves upon the ice | It migrates overland between seas during the winter
exam: Ascertain the number of blowholes possessed by a baleen whale, and their nature. | Two, being nostrils situated upon the top of the head | One, being a mouth for the intake of air | Three, being organs for the emission of song | None, respiration occurring entirely through the skin of the back
